Acceptance

In order to get any licence under these terms, you must agree to them as both strict obligations and conditions to all your licences.

Copyright Licence

The licensor grants you a copyright licence for the software to do everything you might do with the software that would otherwise infringe the licensor's copyright in it for any permitted use. However, you may only distribute the software according to Distribution Licence and make changes or new works based on the software according to Changes and New Works Licence.

Distribution Licence

The licensor grants you an additional copyright licence to distribute copies of the software, as Your Copies allows. Your licence to distribute covers distributing the software with changes and new works permitted by Changes and New Works Licence.

Notices

You must ensure that anyone who gets a copy of any part of the software from you also gets a copy of these terms or the URL for them above, as well as copies of any plain-text lines beginning with `Required Notice:` that the licensor provided with the software. The licensor provides this line:

> Required Notice: Copyright 2026 Hameed, under the Winktank name.

When Winktank is a legal person that holds the copyright in the software, the licensor will provide this line in its place:

> Required Notice: Copyright {year} {Winktank's registered name}, {where it is registered and its number}.

Changes and New Works Licence

The licensor grants you an additional copyright licence to make changes and new works based on the software for any permitted use.

Patent Licence

The licensor grants you a patent licence for the software that covers patent claims the licensor can license, or becomes able to license, that you would infringe by using the software.

Who May Use

Any natural person may use the software, for any purpose, in anything they do with their own hands.

That includes work others pay you for.

If you build a client's thing with the software as a freelancer working on your own account, that is a permitted use, and so is charging for it.

It also includes making things to sell, building a product or a business of your own, and using the software for your own projects while someone else employs you, as long as the use is not for your employer's work.

Who May Not

These terms give no licence to any organisation, of any size.

They give no licence to any person for use while acting for an organisation.

A company or other legal entity with a single member is an organisation, and using the software for its work is acting for it, even when its only member does all the work with their own hands.

If something you made as a person becomes the business of an organisation, your use of the software for that organisation from then on is not a permitted use, and what you made before is still yours to keep, sell or give to it.

What You Make Is Yours

What you make with the software is yours: what it produces for you, the projects you build with it, and the records it keeps of your work.

The licensor claims nothing in any of it.

These terms put no limit on what you do with what you make, who you give it to, or who you sell it to, organisations included.

Where the software writes part of its own text into something you make, such as a template or a starting file, that text is part of what you make, and the licensor claims nothing in it either.

That does not cover copying the software itself, or a working part of it, into what you make: a copy of the software stays under these terms.

These terms do not decide whether the law protects what you make, or what rights others have in material you bring into it.

What you make stays yours if your licences end.

Your Copies

You may give copies of the software, changed or not, to other people under these same terms.

Each person who gets a copy gets their own licence from the licensor under these terms, as far as Who May Use and Who May Not allow, and not from you.

Putting a copy where anyone can download it, such as a public repository, counts as giving copies to people.

You may not install the software in an organisation's systems, or hand a copy to an organisation for its use.

Organisations

An organisation that wants to use the software, or a person who wants to use it for an organisation, may write to the licensor at hi@winktank.org and ask for a separate licence.

Nothing in these terms grants an organisation anything, including an organisation that got a copy from someone else.

Nothing in these terms promises that a separate licence will be offered, or on what terms.

Fair Use

You may have "fair use" rights for the software under the law. These terms do not limit them.

No Other Rights

These terms do not allow you to sublicense or transfer any of your licences to anyone else, or prevent the licensor from granting licences to anyone else. These terms do not imply any other licences.

Patent Defence

If you make any written claim that the software infringes or contributes to infringement of any patent, your patent licence for the software granted under these terms ends immediately. If your company makes such a claim, your patent licence ends immediately for work on behalf of your company.

Violations

The first time you are notified in writing that you have violated any of these terms, or done anything with the software not covered by your licences, your licences can nonetheless continue if you come into full compliance with these terms, and take practical steps to correct past violations, within 32 days of receiving notice. Otherwise, all your licences end immediately.

No Liability

As far as the law allows, the software comes as is, without any warranty or condition, and the licensor will not be liable to you for any damages arising out of these terms or the use or nature of the software, under any kind of legal claim.

Definitions

The licensor is Hameed, offering these terms under the Winktank name, until Winktank is a legal person that holds the copyright in the software; from then on, the licensor is that legal person.

The software is the software the licensor makes available under these terms.

You refers to the natural person agreeing to these terms.

A natural person is a human being, not a company or any other legal entity.

Your company is any legal entity, sole proprietorship, or other kind of organisation that you work for, plus all organisations that have control over, are under the control of, or are under common control with that organisation. Control means ownership of substantially all the assets of an entity, or the power to direct its management and policies by vote, contract, or otherwise. Control can be direct or indirect.

An organisation is any company, partnership, cooperative, trust, foundation, charity, association, club, school, university, research body, government or public body, or any other legal entity or group organised to act together, of any size, whether or not it is incorporated and whether or not it seeks a profit, together with every organisation that controls it, is controlled by it, or is under common control with it.

A natural person who trades on their own account, in their own name or under a business name, with no separate legal entity, is not an organisation.

Acting for an organisation means using the software as its employee for its work, or as a contractor, agent or volunteer working under its direction, in its systems, or in its name.

Making something with your own copy, on your own account, and handing what you made to an organisation is not acting for it.

With your own hands means work you do or direct yourself, including work the software or a model does at your direction.

A permitted use is a use that Who May Use allows and Who May Not does not take away.

Your licences are all the licences granted to you for the software under these terms.

Use means anything you do with the software requiring one of your licences.
